{{also|Action}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂eǵ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|accion}}, from {{der|en|fro|aucion}}, {{m|fro|acciun}}, from {{der|en|la|āctiō||act of doing or making}}, from {{m|la|āctus}} + action suffix {{m|la|-iō}}, perfect passive participle of {{m|la|agō|agere|do, act}}, ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₂éǵeti}}; see also {{m|en|act}}, {{m|en|active}}.

Morphologically {{suffix|en|act|ion}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈæk.ʃən/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-action.ogg|a=US}}
* {{hyphenation|en|ac|tion}}
<!-- 2 syllable words !-->
* {{rhymes|en|ækʃən|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The [[effort]] of [[performing]] or [[doing]] something.
# Something [[done]], often so as to accomplish a [[purpose]].
#: {{cot|en|occurrence|state of being|q1=what verbs can express}}
# A way of [[motion]] or functioning.
#: {{ux|en|Knead bread with a rocking '''action'''.}}
# Fast-paced [[activity]].
#: {{ux|en|a movie full of exciting '''action'''}}
# The way in which a [[mechanical]] [[device]] acts when used; especially a [[firearm]].
#: {{ux|en|Pressing a piano key causes the '''action''' of the hammer on the string.}}
## {{lb|en|firearms}} The way in which [[cartridge]]s are [[load]]ed, [[lock]]ed, and [[extract]]ed from the [[mechanism]].
##: {{co|en|pump-'''action''' shotgun}}
# {{lb|en|music}} The [[mechanism]], that is the set of moving mechanical parts, of a [[keyboard]] instrument, like a [[piano]], which transfers the motion of the [[key]] to the sound-making [[device]].<ref>Marshall Cavendish Corporation [http://books.google.com/books?id=pxgiFygAblQC&pg=PA1079 ''Growing Up with Science''] p.1079</ref>
#: {{ux|en|The run in bar 12 is almost impossible with this piano's heavy '''action'''.}}
# {{lb|en|music|lutherie}} The distance separating the [[string]]s and the [[fingerboard]] on a [[string instrument]].
#: {{ux|en|You're getting fret buzz because the '''action''' is too low.}}
# {{lb|en|slang|typically with a quantifier}} [[sexual intercourse|Sexual intercourse]].
#: {{ux|en|She gave him some '''action'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|I hope to get a bit of '''action''' with the hot guy from the club.}}
# {{lb|en|military}} [[combat|Combat]].
#: {{ux|en|He saw some '''action''' in the Korean War.}}
# {{lb|en|legal}} A [[charge]] or other process in a law court (also called {{m|en|lawsuit}} and ''[[actio]]'').
# {{lb|en|mathematics}} A way in which each [[element]] of some [[algebraic structure]] [[transform|transforms]] some other [[structure]] or [[set]], in a way which [[respect|respects]] the structure of the first. Formally, this may be seen as a [[morphism]] from the first structure into some structure of [[endomorphism|endomorphisms]] of the second; for example, a [[group action]] of a [[group]] ''G'' on a set ''S'' can be seen as a group [[homomorphism]] from ''G'' into the set of [[bijection|bijections]] on ''S'' (which form a group under function [[composition]]), while a [[module]] ''M'' over a [[ring]] ''R'' can be defined as an [[abelian group]] together with a [[ring homomorphism]] from ''R'' into the ring of group endomorphisms of ''M'' (which is also called the ''action'' of ''R'' on ''M'').
# {{senseid|en|physics}} {{lb|en|physics}} The product of [[energy]] and [[time]], especially the product of the [[Lagrangian function|Lagrangian]] and time.
# {{lb|en|literature}} The event or connected series of events, either real or imaginary, forming the subject of a play, poem, or other composition; the unfolding of the drama of events.
# {{lb|en|art|painting and sculpture}} The attitude or position of the several parts of the body as expressive of the sentiment or passion depicted.
# {{lb|en|bowling}} [[spin]] put on the bowling ball.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[share]] in the [[capital]] [[stock]] of a joint-stock company, or in the public funds.
#* {{RQ:Smollett Peregrine Pickle|volume=IV|106|text=So saying he presented him with two '''actions''' of above two thousand livres each.}}
#* {{RQ:Burke Revolution in France|passage=the Euripus of funds and '''actions'''}}
# {{lb|en|Christianity}} A religious performance or solemn function, i.e. [[action sermon]], a sacramental sermon in the Scots Presbyterian Church.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Duncan B. Forrester; Doug Gay|title=Worship and Liturgy in Context|publisher=scm Press|page=88
|passage=The '''Action''' Sermon is quite simply, then, the eucharistic sermon.}}
# {{lb|en|science}} A process existing in or produced by nature (rather than by the intent of human beings).
# {{lb|en|Misesian praxeology|[[w:Austrian school of economics|Austrian economics]]}} [[purposeful|Purposeful]] behavior.
# A demonstration by [[activist]]s.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=11 Apr 87|journal=Gay Community News|page=1|author=Kim Westheimer|title=NY Protesters Rip FDA|text=The protest was set up in less than three weeks by an ad hoc group called Act Up {{...}} According to Kramer, Act Up will continue AIDS-related organizing, possibly inclcuding an '''action''' at an early June Washington, D.C., AIDS conference for scientists worldwide.}}

